WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.790
This is an agent that publishes to my site. I'm going to start by asking it to publish something that isn't finished.

2
00:00:14.976 --> 00:00:19.091
One tool call. Validation.

3
00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:27.000
You can see here, there are three kinds of problem.

4
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:32.900
Two required fields missing, four recommended, and a third category: my own draft TODOs.

5
00:00:32.948 --> 00:00:37.544
Those ride into the RSS feed's content, so a note to myself would have shipped to everyone subscribed.

6
00:00:38.939 --> 00:00:44.900
And we can see here, nothing happened. HEAD, my local main, origin, all sitting on one commit;

7
00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:50.900
a feed rebuild from before I started. No commit, no push, nothing written to the corpus;

8
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.920
the chain stops at the first failure.

9
00:00:56.911 --> 00:01:03.742
Now I'm going to show this to you. Same agent, a post that's finished, and I've left two words out of the sentence.

10
00:01:11.888 --> 00:01:15.880
It ran a dry run. I didn't ask for one; that's the default.

11
00:01:15.883 --> 00:01:20.760
I asked for the raw result, so the JSON is what the tool returned, not the model's description of what it returned.

12
00:01:20.874 --> 00:01:22.874
One entry. Validate.

13
00:01:26.865 --> 00:01:30.380
And we can see the write steps aren't in there at all.

14
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.156
That absence is the gate, not the flag. A flag is a claim; a missing step is evidence.

15
00:01:38.846 --> 00:01:43.802
You can see here, the post is still sitting there uncommitted. To make this real, I have to say so.

16
00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:05.800
There it is. That's not a prompt instruction the model might drift off; it's a function signature.

17
00:02:05.805 --> 00:02:08.565
The dangerous path costs an extra argument.

18
00:02:23.777 --> 00:02:29.760
Now for real. This pushes to a live site.

19
00:02:29.768 --> 00:02:35.643
One decision from the model: publish, or don't. Everything after this is code.

20
00:02:38.754 --> 00:02:41.740
Validate, commit and push.

21
00:02:41.749 --> 00:02:45.740
Then it polls the live URL until it returns 200,

22
00:02:45.745 --> 00:02:50.730
because the page takes a moment to deploy and I don't want the next step running against a 404.

23
00:02:50.735 --> 00:02:53.720
We can see — no warnings.

24
00:02:53.731 --> 00:02:56.720
The second-wave jobs had already caught up.

25
00:02:56.726 --> 00:02:59.006
But I'll show you what happens when they haven't.

26
00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:04.797
At the top of my blog, we can see the new post was absent.

27
00:03:05.712 --> 00:03:08.700
But I've just run the new job and I've refreshed the page,

28
00:03:08.708 --> 00:03:10.148
so now the post is live.

29
00:03:17.694 --> 00:03:25.684
Everything you just watched runs against my site, which you can't inspect. So the repo ships this.

30
00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:32.670
No API key, no credentials, no network.

31
00:03:32.671 --> 00:03:38.660
It builds two git repositories and their bare remotes in a temp directory,

32
00:03:38.666 --> 00:03:42.180
serves them over a local HTTP server, and runs the same chain.

33
00:03:42.657 --> 00:03:47.152
Real commits, real pushes, real polling; only the destination is disposable.

34
00:03:47.647 --> 00:03:53.630
Same four acts. It refuses; it defaults to dry run.

35
00:03:53.638 --> 00:03:59.620
There. The sitemap hasn't listed the post yet and the share image isn't rendered yet,

36
00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:02.600
so it says both out loud; and it publishes anyway.

37
00:04:02.624 --> 00:04:07.220
Then the same run with the second-wave jobs finishing in time, and it goes quiet.

38
00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:12.110
Gate it, and a slow build refuses a perfectly good post.

39
00:04:20.597 --> 00:04:27.587
CI runs the suite on two Python versions, then runs that demo as its own job.

40
00:04:29.583 --> 00:04:33.898
Three jobs. Different machine, every push.

41
00:04:35.573 --> 00:04:43.224
“Try it yourself” is a claim about someone else's computer, and the only way to make that claim honest was to run it on someone else's machine.

42
00:05:17.509 --> 00:05:23.480
The refusal behaviour is pinned.

43
00:05:23.499 --> 00:05:26.530
The file's own docstring calls them the thesis tests.

44
00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.495
If this one goes red, the project has stopped doing the thing it says it does.

45
00:06:05.435 --> 00:06:10.930
One more, and I only found it because I wrote about it.

46
00:06:11.425 --> 00:06:17.061
The first of these proves the check warns when the image is late; that was always there.

47
00:06:17.416 --> 00:06:22.652
The third proves it goes quiet when the image arrives on time; and that one wasn't.

48
00:06:23.407 --> 00:06:29.898
The sitemap had both halves. The image had one, and I'd been reading the sitemap's pair as if it covered both.

49
00:06:38.384 --> 00:06:41.370
Same rule, second place.

50
00:06:41.379 --> 00:06:45.895
Configuration gets checked before the commit, not when stage five finally needs it.

51
00:06:50.365 --> 00:06:54.801
A run that's already doomed must never take the irreversible step first.

52
00:07:05.342 --> 00:07:11.330
A missing path is a defect, so it's a gate.

53
00:07:11.333 --> 00:07:14.320
A lagging build is a queue, so it's a warning.

54
00:07:14.328 --> 00:07:16.328
Waiting fixes one of those, and never the other.

55
00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:29.300
One decision from the model.

56
00:07:29.305 --> 00:07:32.290
Everything else deterministic, ordered, stopping at the first failure;

57
00:07:32.301 --> 00:07:36.296
with exactly one place where it's lenient, and a reason I can defend.

58
00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:41.787
Gate what you're judging; warn about what you're waiting on.

